6. Requirements, Risks and Constraints
Functional Requirements
The solution must:
•	Ingest and validate the approved retail dataset.
•	Clean and prepare transaction data.
•	Create customer-level behavioural features.
•	Calculate RFM measures.
•	Develop and evaluate customer segments.
•	Profile and interpret each segment.
•	Develop predictive analysis for customer inactivity/churn.
•	Analyse demand patterns.
•	Generate segment-specific recommendations.
•	Present results through an interactive dashboard.
•	Document analytical assumptions and limitations.
Non-Functional Requirements
The solution should be:
•	Reproducible: Results should be reproducible using documented code and data-processing procedures.
•	Usable: Outputs should be understandable to non-technical stakeholders.
•	Secure: Access to customer-level information should be controlled.
•	Traceable: Analytical outputs should be traceable to source data and documented transformations.
•	Maintainable: Code should follow a clear and modular repository structure.
•	Scalable: The architecture should support larger datasets in future implementations.
•	Transparent: Users should understand what the models do and do not predict.
Key Risks and Constraints
Potential project constraints include:
•	Missing or incomplete customer identifiers.
•	Cancelled transactions and unusual transaction values.
•	Historical rather than real-time data.
•	Limited demographic information.
•	Potential changes in customer behaviour over time.
•	Difficulty determining the optimal number of customer clusters.
•	Risk of interpreting clusters as fixed customer identities.
•	Limited ability to assess demographic fairness where protected-group data is unavailable.
The project will address these limitations through data-quality checks, sensitivity analysis, appropriate validation techniques, clear documentation, and stakeholder review.

7. Ethical Risk Register
Ethical Risk	Potential Impact	Mitigation
Bias in segmentation	Certain customer groups may be poorly represented or incorrectly classified	Evaluate segment characteristics and model performance
Privacy violation	Customer information could be unnecessarily exposed	Data minimization, access controls, and pseudonymization
Misuse of customer segments	Segments could be used to unfairly exclude customers	Establish acceptable-use guidelines
Over-reliance on predictions	Managers may treat predictions as certain outcomes	Provide confidence/error information and human oversight
Unfair targeting	Some customers could receive inappropriate or excessive marketing	Establish segment-specific targeting rules and monitoring
Lack of transparency	Users may not understand how segments were created	Document RFM calculations, clustering methodology, and assumptions
Regulatory non-compliance	Improper processing of personal data could create legal/reputational risk	Apply data governance and privacy review

8. Quality, Documentation and Governance Standards
Quality assurance will be incorporated throughout the project rather than only at the final modelling stage.
Data quality checks will include missing-value analysis, duplicate detection, validation of transaction quantities and prices, cancelled-transaction assessment, customer-level reconciliation, and identification of unusual observations.
The segmentation process will evaluate cluster quality using appropriate statistical measures and business interpretation. Different clustering configurations will be compared rather than selecting a solution solely because it produces a visually appealing result.
Predictive models will be evaluated using appropriate performance measures and validated using methods that account for the temporal nature of transaction data where applicable. Explainability and error analysis will accompany model performance evaluation.
Documentation Standards
The project will maintain:
•	Data dictionary
•	Data-quality report
•	Feature documentation
•	Segmentation methodology
•	Model documentation
•	Model evaluation report
•	Ethical risk register
•	Decision log
•	Dashboard documentation
•	Project README
•	Version history
This documentation will provide traceability from the original transaction data through data preparation, segmentation, predictive modelling, and final recommendations.
Versioning Strategy
Git/GitHub will be used for source-code and documentation version control. A feature-branch workflow will be used during development, with tagged releases for major milestones.
DVC (Data Version Control) will be used to track large datasets and model artefacts without storing large raw files directly in Git.
Models will be versioned according to their:
•	Training data period
•	Feature set
•	Algorithm
•	Hyperparameters
•	Performance results
•	Fairness assessment
•	Explainability assessment
•	Approval status
Data Governance
The project will apply the principles of purpose limitation, data minimization, accuracy, storage limitation, security, and accountability. These principles are consistent with established data-protection guidance from the Information Commissioner's Office (ICO, 2026).
Customer identifiers will be restricted to legitimate analytical requirements. Where customer-level information is displayed, pseudonymized or aggregated information will be preferred. Data will not be retained longer than necessary for the approved analytical purpose.

9. Project Success Criteria
The project will be considered successful if it produces:
1.	Clearly differentiated and interpretable customer segments.
2.	Evidence that the segments provide meaningful business insight.
3.	Reliable predictive insights concerning customer inactivity and demand.
4.	Actionable recommendations for each major customer segment.
5.	An interactive dashboard suitable for business stakeholders.
6.	Documented ethical safeguards and limitations.
7.	Reproducible analytical workflows.
8.	A technical foundation that can be extended through Modules 2–5 and the Final Project.
The ultimate measure of success is not simply the accuracy of a clustering or predictive model. The project should demonstrate that customer segmentation can help the retailer make better-informed decisions about where to allocate marketing, retention, and operational resources.
